      "locked": false,
      "text": "Recovery:",
      "fontSize": 17.621569881462683,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Recovery:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "H_bKYFDxNV9-giKoiXF_N",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 128.17195248905585,
      "y": 4112.492929495519,
      "width": 691.928139204617,
      "height": 44.05392470365671,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2U",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1226478194,
      "version": 219,
      "versionNonce": 562556018,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782242284047,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Exponential Backoff: The worker schedules the next retry further in the future\n(jittered to prevent thundering herds).",
      "fontSize": 17.621569881462683,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Exponential Backoff: The worker schedules the next retry further in the future (jittered to prevent thundering herds).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "KCK-qZVqpf8pxv95329tD",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 145.83764620501483,
      "y": 4154.344157963993,
      "width": 656.596751772699,
      "height": 66.08088705548506,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1499694510,
      "version": 219,
      "versionNonce": 210815918,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782242284047,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Circuit Breaker: If a specific destination fails 100% of requests, the circuit\nbreaker opens, instantly failing future tasks for a short period to save\nresources.",
      "fontSize": 17.621569881462683,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Circuit Breaker: If a specific destination fails 100% of requests, the circuit breaker opens, instantly failing future tasks for a short period to save resources.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "DMvlLw55clTSZOnCDShSd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 124.44503455607261,
      "y": 4240.249311136124,
      "width": 699.3819750705834,
      "height": 44.05392470365671,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2W",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 79918130,
      "version": 219,
      "versionNonce": 1517653554,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782242284047,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Dead Letter Queue (DLQ): After max retries (e.g., 72 hours), the event is moved\nto a DLQ for manual inspection, preventing head-of-line blocking.",
      "fontSize": 17.621569881462683,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Dead Letter Queue (DLQ): After max retries (e.g., 72 hours), the event is moved to a DLQ for manual inspection, preventing head-of-line blocking.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "LS37-0LvaT1Pk8wWGN84e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 125.98920939583104,
      "y": 1549.1103334047707,
      "width": 2818.32470703125,
      "height": 2006.1284955934261,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2X",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1149839794,
      "version": 327,
      "versionNonce": 2086099981,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785898634985,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\n-- Control Plane (Registration): Manages webhook subscriptions. Needs high availability (99.99%) and global reach.\n-- Storage: Multi-region KV/NoSQL (e.g., DynamoDB Global Tables, Spanner) for active-active config.\n-- Data Plane (Ingestion & Delivery): Handles the massive stream of events and delivery attempts.\n-- Ingestion: Kafka (Events Topic).\n-- Routing: Redis Cluster (Inverted Index: event_type -> webhook_ids).\n-- Task Queue: Kafka (Delivery Topic, keyed by webhook_id).\n-- State Tracking: Scalable KV Store (e.g., Cassandra/DynamoDB) for delivery status.\n\n\n\n\n\n1. Request: Client sends POST /webhooks with an Idempotency-Key and JWT.\n2. Persist: The API (in the nearest region) writes the config to the multi-region store (Source of Truth).\n3. Secret Gen: A signing secret is generated/rotated in KMS.\n4. Propagate: The system publishes a \"config-change\" event.\n5. Index: Indexer services in all regions consume the change and update the Redis Inverted Index (event_type -> [webhook_id_1, webhook_id_2]).\n\n\n\n1. Ingest: Producers send events to the Events Topic in Kafka (keyed by event_type or tenant_id).\n2. Process & Route:\n--- The Event Processor consumes the event.\n-- It looks up matching subscribers in the Redis Inverted Index.\n-- Fan-out: For every match, it creates a delivery task.\n----- Enqueue: The processor publishes tasks to the Deliveries Topic in Kafka, keyed by webhook_id (ensures sequential processing per destination).\n----- Deliver:\n- Delivery Workers consume tasks.\n- They check per-client rate limits (token bucket).\n- They sign the payload (HMAC with timestamp + body).\n- They send the HTTP POST to the external destination.\n1/ Result Handling:\n** Success (2xx): Mark as delivered in the Delivery State KV.\n** Retryable (429/5xx/Timeout): Update next_attempt_at in KV; push to a delay queue/scheduler.\n** Failure (4xx): Mark as failed (no retry).\n\n\n\n\n*** A. The \"Fan-Out\" Problem (Scale)\n-- Challenge: A single event might need to go to 10,000 subscribers. Querying a standard SQL DB for every event is too slow.\n-- Solution: An Inverted Index in Redis.\n-- Structure: Key = event_type, Value = Set<webhook_ids>.\n-- Optimization: For \"hot\" event types (e.g., \"user_update\"), shard the key into event_type:0 ... event_type:N to spread the read load across the Redis cluster.\n\n*** B. Idempotency & Durability\n-- Events: Kafka is the immutable log/source of truth.\n-- Delivery State: A dedicated KV store tracks (webhook_id, event_id) tuples.\n-- Uses conditional writes to ensure a delivery is never processed twice (even if a worker crashes and restarts).\n-- Client Side: The API accepts an Idempotency-Key so clients can safely retry registration requests without creating duplicate webhooks.\n\n\n\n*** Scenario 1: Redis (Cache) Failure\nProblem: The Redis cluster holding the subscription index goes down or loses data.\nRecovery: The Event Processor detects a connection failure or \"stale version\" error. It falls back to querying the Multi-region Config Store (slower, but accurate) to route events while a background process rebuilds the Redis cache from the source of truth.\n------\n\n*** Scenario 2: Region Outage\nProblem: An entire AWS/GCP region goes dark.\nRecovery (Control Plane): DNS (Route53) fails over to the next nearest region. Since the database is a Multi-region Global Table, the new region already has the data and can accept writes.\nRecovery (Data Plane): Kafka topics are mirrored (e.g., MirrorMaker 2). Workers in the healthy region pick up the slack, consuming from the replicated topics.\n-------\n\n**** Scenario 3: Destination Downtime\nProblem: The user's server is crashing (returning 500s).\nRecovery:\nExponential Backoff: The worker schedules the next retry further in the future (jittered to prevent thundering herds).\nCircuit Breaker: If a specific destination fails 100% of requests, the circuit breaker opens, instantly failing future tasks for a short period to save resources.\nDead Letter Queue (DLQ): After max retries (e.g., 72 hours), the event is moved to a DLQ for manual inspection, preventing head-of-line blocking.",
      "fontSize": 22.290316617704736,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n-- Control Plane (Registration): Manages webhook subscriptions. Needs high availability (99.99%) and global reach.\n-- Storage: Multi-region KV/NoSQL (e.g., DynamoDB Global Tables, Spanner) for active-active config.\n-- Data Plane (Ingestion & Delivery): Handles the massive stream of events and delivery attempts.\n-- Ingestion: Kafka (Events Topic).\n-- Routing: Redis Cluster (Inverted Index: event_type -> webhook_ids).\n-- Task Queue: Kafka (Delivery Topic, keyed by webhook_id).\n-- State Tracking: Scalable KV Store (e.g., Cassandra/DynamoDB) for delivery status.\n\n\n\n\n\n1. Request: Client sends POST /webhooks with an Idempotency-Key and JWT.\n2. Persist: The API (in the nearest region) writes the config to the multi-region store (Source of Truth).\n3. Secret Gen: A signing secret is generated/rotated in KMS.\n4. Propagate: The system publishes a \"config-change\" event.\n5. Index: Indexer services in all regions consume the change and update the Redis Inverted Index (event_type -> [webhook_id_1, webhook_id_2]).\n\n\n\n1. Ingest: Producers send events to the Events Topic in Kafka (keyed by event_type or tenant_id).\n2. Process & Route:\n--- The Event Processor consumes the event.\n-- It looks up matching subscribers in the Redis Inverted Index.\n-- Fan-out: For every match, it creates a delivery task.\n----- Enqueue: The processor publishes tasks to the Deliveries Topic in Kafka, keyed by webhook_id (ensures sequential processing per destination).\n----- Deliver:\n- Delivery Workers consume tasks.\n- They check per-client rate limits (token bucket).\n- They sign the payload (HMAC with timestamp + body).\n- They send the HTTP POST to the external destination.\n1/ Result Handling:\n** Success (2xx): Mark as delivered in the Delivery State KV.\n** Retryable (429/5xx/Timeout): Update next_attempt_at in KV; push to a delay queue/scheduler.\n** Failure (4xx): Mark as failed (no retry).\n\n\n\n\n*** A. The \"Fan-Out\" Problem (Scale)\n-- Challenge: A single event might need to go to 10,000 subscribers. Querying a standard SQL DB for every event is too slow.\n-- Solution: An Inverted Index in Redis.\n-- Structure: Key = event_type, Value = Set<webhook_ids>.\n-- Optimization: For \"hot\" event types (e.g., \"user_update\"), shard the key into event_type:0 ... event_type:N to spread the read load across the Redis cluster.\n\n*** B. Idempotency & Durability\n-- Events: Kafka is the immutable log/source of truth.\n-- Delivery State: A dedicated KV store tracks (webhook_id, event_id) tuples.\n-- Uses conditional writes to ensure a delivery is never processed twice (even if a worker crashes and restarts).\n-- Client Side: The API accepts an Idempotency-Key so clients can safely retry registration requests without creating duplicate webhooks.\n\n\n\n*** Scenario 1: Redis (Cache) Failure\nProblem: The Redis cluster holding the subscription index goes down or loses data.\nRecovery: The Event Processor detects a connection failure or \"stale version\" error. It falls back to querying the Multi-region Config Store (slower, but accurate) to route events while a background process rebuilds the Redis cache from the source of truth.\n------\n\n*** Scenario 2: Region Outage\nProblem: An entire AWS/GCP region goes dark.\nRecovery (Control Plane): DNS (Route53) fails over to the next nearest region. Since the database is a Multi-region Global Table, the new region already has the data and can accept writes.\nRecovery (Data Plane): Kafka topics are mirrored (e.g., MirrorMaker 2). Workers in the healthy region pick up the slack, consuming from the replicated topics.\n-------\n\n**** Scenario 3: Destination Downtime\nProblem: The user's server is crashing (returning 500s).\nRecovery:\nExponential Backoff: The worker schedules the next retry further in the future (jittered to prevent thundering herds).\nCircuit Breaker: If a specific destination fails 100% of requests, the circuit breaker opens, instantly failing future tasks for a short period to save resources.\nDead Letter Queue (DLQ): After max retries (e.g., 72 hours), the event is moved to a DLQ for manual inspection, preventing head-of-line blocking.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
